---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { specDrivenArticle as article } from '../../data/writing';
---
<BaseLayout title={`${article.title} | Haseeb Sultan`} description={article.description} article={{ published: article.published, image: article.image, headline: article.title }}>
  <Header />
  <main id="main" class="shell article-page">
    <article aria-labelledby="article-title">
      <figure class="article-cover">
        <img src={article.image} alt="Spec-Driven Development: Engineering mature software with AI coding agents. Tangled orange lines resolve into a structured architectural blueprint." width="2296" height="1196" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
      </figure>
      <header class="article-heading reading-column">
        <a href="/writing/" class="article-back">← All writing</a>
        <p class="eyebrow">{article.category}</p>
        <h1 id="article-title">{article.title}</h1>
        <p class="article-deck">{article.description}</p>
        <p class="article-byline">Haseeb Sultan <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> <time datetime={article.published}>September 20, 2026</time></p>
      </header>
      <div class="article-body reading-column">
        <p>AI coding agents can turn a short request into a working implementation remarkably quickly. The harder question is whether that implementation belongs in the system we actually want to build.</p>
        <p>A feature can work in isolation while making the wider application harder to maintain. One component may introduce a new database, another may bypass an existing authorization rule, and a third may duplicate a service that already exists. Each change can appear reasonable when judged only against the prompt that produced it.</p>
        <p>Spec-driven development gives this work a shared reference point: a written account of the intended behavior, constraints, and conditions for success. Code is then planned, implemented, and reviewed against that intent.</p>

        <nav class="article-contents" aria-label="In this article">
          <p>In this article</p>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#from-prompts-to-specifications">From prompts to specifications</a></li>
            <li><a href="#project-constitution">The project constitution</a></li>
            <li><a href="#human-and-agent">The human architect and the AI builder</a></li>
            <li><a href="#putting-it-into-practice">Putting the approach into practice</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <section aria-labelledby="from-prompts-to-specifications">
          <h2 id="from-prompts-to-specifications">1. From prompts to specifications</h2>
          <p>Prompt-first development is useful for exploration. It helps us test an idea, sketch an interface, or discover what a tool can do. Problems emerge when a growing production system depends on decisions scattered across conversations that nobody maintains.</p>
          <p>This creates <strong>architectural drift</strong>: individual changes gradually pull the software away from its intended design. The agent may satisfy the latest request while missing a decision made earlier about service boundaries, data ownership, or compatibility.</p>
          <p>In a spec-driven workflow, the specification becomes the reference for intended behavior. It records what should happen, why it matters, what is outside scope, and how the result will be accepted. The implementation remains evidence of what the system actually does; tests and review connect the two.</p>
          <p>A specification can guide an agent and supply acceptance criteria for automated checks. That does not make every prose document directly executable, or guarantee that generated code is correct. The useful change is that requirements can be examined and corrected before they spread through the implementation.</p>

          <h3>Small decisions can have a large reach</h3>
          <p>Consider a proposed move from SQLite to MongoDB. Updating the specification makes the intended change visible to the whole team, and an agent can help identify its consequences across persistence code, API handlers, tests, and deployment configuration.</p>
          <p>The database names alone are not a migration plan. The specification still needs to address the data model, query behavior, consistency expectations, migration steps, and rollback. An agent can assist with the work once those decisions are clear, but the change must be validated against real requirements.</p>
          <p>The value is coordination: one reviewed decision can guide many related edits. Recording its rationale also preserves the “why” for the next person or agent working on the system.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="project-constitution">
          <h2 id="project-constitution">2. The project constitution</h2>
          <p>A feature specification explains a particular change. A project constitution records the principles that should guide changes throughout the project. It gives the team a durable reference when a chat ends, an agent changes, or a new contributor joins.</p>
          <p>A useful starting point is to organize that foundation around three concerns:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Mission:</strong> who the system serves, the problem it solves, and the boundaries of its scope.</li>
            <li><strong>Technical constraints:</strong> the architectural decisions the team has agreed to follow, such as TypeScript, approved data stores, service boundaries, and testing expectations.</li>
            <li><strong>Roadmap:</strong> the sequence of outcomes the team intends to deliver. Keep this linked to the constitution, with milestones that can evolve without constantly rewriting enduring principles.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>These are an organizing approach, not a universal file format. In GitHub Spec Kit, the constitution establishes project principles, while feature specifications and implementation plans describe individual changes. Its workflow separates requirements, planning, tasks, and implementation. <a href="https://github.github.com/spec-kit/reference/agentic-sdd.html">See the Spec Kit workflow reference.</a></p>
          <p>Keep the constitution specific enough to influence a decision. “Build high-quality software” offers little direction. “Preserve existing public API contracts, test authorization boundaries, and include a rollback plan for database migrations” gives reviewers something concrete to check.</p>

          <h3>Starting fresh or working with an existing system</h3>
          <p>For a <strong>greenfield project</strong>, define the mission, constraints, and initial milestones before asking an agent to build substantial functionality. Start with the decisions needed for the first useful slice and refine them as the project develops.</p>
          <p>For a <strong>brownfield project</strong>, begin with the repository. Inspect dependencies, folder structure, interfaces, tests, and existing architecture decisions. Distinguish established behavior from proposed improvements before writing new rules. GitHub’s guidance for existing projects recommends capturing real constraints and starting with a bounded change rather than retrospectively specifying the entire system. <a href="https://github.github.io/spec-kit/guides/existing-projects.html">See the existing-project adoption guide.</a></p>

          <h3>How the constitution relates to AGENTS.md</h3>
          <p><code>AGENTS.md</code> provides repository instructions for coding agents. It commonly covers setup commands, tests, coding conventions, and project-specific context. It is an open format that can be shared across compatible agents, rather than a file tied to one vendor. <a href="https://agents.md/">See the AGENTS.md format.</a></p>
          <p>A constitution expresses the wider agreement about the product and its engineering principles. <code>AGENTS.md</code> explains how an agent should work within that agreement. The two can overlap: an instruction file may include architecture rules or link to the constitution. Keep their responsibilities clear and avoid contradictory copies of the same rule.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="human-and-agent">
          <h2 id="human-and-agent">3. The human architect and the AI builder</h2>
          <p>AI-assisted engineering shifts more attention toward intent, boundaries, and judgment. Someone must still decide which problem is worth solving, which trade-offs are acceptable, and what evidence is needed before a change is released.</p>
          <p>The architect-and-builder analogy is useful here, even when one developer fills several roles. A blueprint gives the builder dimensions, materials, and constraints. In software, that means requirements, interfaces, architectural decisions, and acceptance criteria.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The human owns the decisions:</strong> product intent, business context, architecture, risk tolerance, and acceptance of the result. The team remains responsible for reviewing and operating what it ships.</li>
            <li><strong>The agent helps execute:</strong> repository analysis, implementation planning, code generation, refactoring, testing, and documentation within the agreed scope.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>An agent can propose a trade-off and explain its reasoning. That proposal still needs to be evaluated against the context and constraints of the project. Producing an implementation quickly does not establish that the underlying decision was appropriate.</p>
          <p>This is the distinction between <strong>implementation autonomy</strong> and <strong>decision autonomy</strong>. Give agents enough room to carry out well-defined work, and make clear which decisions require human judgment. More detailed instructions are useful when they describe outcomes and constraints; prescribing every line of code can obscure the problem we are trying to solve.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="putting-it-into-practice">
          <h2 id="putting-it-into-practice">4. Putting the approach into practice</h2>
          <p>A practical spec-first loop can stay small:</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>State the outcome.</strong> Describe the user’s problem, expected behavior, and scope boundaries.</li>
            <li><strong>Make uncertainty visible.</strong> Resolve the assumptions that could change the design.</li>
            <li><strong>Plan against the system.</strong> Identify affected interfaces, data, dependencies, and constraints.</li>
            <li><strong>Build a reviewable increment.</strong> Give the agent a bounded task with clear acceptance criteria.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the result.</strong> Use tests, code review, and appropriate operational checks to compare the implementation with the specification.</li>
            <li><strong>Reconcile what changed.</strong> Update the relevant specification and decision records when implementation reveals something new.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>The amount of documentation should match the decision. A small change may need a few explicit requirements. A migration or a new subsystem needs deeper treatment of interfaces, failure modes, and rollout.</p>
          <p>Spec-driven development is most useful when it keeps intent visible throughout delivery. The specification guides the work, the implementation exposes gaps, and review brings them back into agreement. AI can accelerate that loop; people still determine what successful engineering means.</p>
        </section>
        <a href="/writing/" class="text-link">Explore more writing →</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-page { padding-block: 40px 76px; }
  .article-cover img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: 8px; }
  .reading-column { max-width: 760px; margin-inline: auto; }
  .article-heading { padding-top: 36px; }
  .article-back { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; color: var(--color-accent); font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 22px; }
  .article-heading h1 { font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 54px); line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 16px; letter-spacing: -.035em; }
  .article-deck { font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-muted); margin-top: 24px; }
  .article-byline { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--color-muted); padding-block: 22px 28px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line); }
  .article-body { font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.9; padding-top: 32px; overflow-wrap: break-word; }
  .article-body p + p { margin-top: 20px; }
  .article-body h2 { font-size: clamp(26px, 4vw, 32px); line-height: 1.35; margin-block: 48px 20px; letter-spacing: -.025em; }
  .article-body h3 { font-size: 22px; margin-block: 30px 14px; }
  .article-body ul, .article-body ol { padding-inline-start: 24px; margin-block: 20px; }
  .article-body ul { list-style: disc; }
  .article-body ol { list-style: decimal; }
  .article-body li + li { margin-top: 12px; }
  .article-body a:not(.text-link) { color: var(--color-accent); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .article-body code { font-size: .9em; padding: 2px 5px; background: #edf1f7; border-radius: 3px; }
  .article-contents { margin-block: 32px; padding: 24px; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: 8px; background: white; font-size: 15px; }
  .article-contents > p { font-weight: 600; }
  .article-contents ol { margin-bottom: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .article-page { padding-block: 24px 48px; }
    .article-heading { padding-top: 24px; }
    .article-deck { font-size: 18px; }
    .article-body { font-size: 16px; }
    .article-contents { padding: 20px; }
  }
</style>
